To find the determinant of the given matrix, we can use the expansion along the first row. The determinant is given by:

\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \end{vmatrix} &= x_1 \begin{vmatrix} y_2 & 1 \\ y_3 & 1 \end{vmatrix} - y_1 \begin{vmatrix} x_2 & 1 \\ x_3 & 1 \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} x_2 & y_2 \\ x_3 & y_3 \end{vmatrix} \\
&= x_1(y_2 - y_3) - y_1(x_2 - x_3) + (x_2y_3 - x_3y_2).
\end{align*}

Now, we need to find the square of this determinant. To do this, we can square the expression above:

\begin{align*}
\left(\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \end{vmatrix}\right)^2 &= \left(x_1(y_2 - y_3) - y_1(x_2 - x_3) + (x_2y_3 - x_3y_2)\right)^2 \\
&= \left(x_1y_2 - x_1y_3 - y_1x_2 + y_1x_3 + x_2y_3 - x_3y_2\right)^2.
\end{align*}

Now, we can expand the square:

\begin{align*}
\left(x_1y_2 - x_1y_3 - y_1x_2 + y_1x_3 + x_2y_3 - x_3y_2\right)^2 &= \left(x_1y_2 - x_1y_3\right)^2 - 2\left(x_1y_2 - x_1y_3\right)\left(y_1x_2 - y_1x_3\right) \\
&+ \left(y_1x_2 - y_1x_3\right)^2 + 2\left(x_1y_2 - x_1y_3\right)\left(x_2y_3 - x_3y_2\right) \\
&+ \left(x_2y_3 - x_3y_2\right)^2.
\end{align*}

Now, we can simplify the expression by expanding the squares and combining like terms:

\begin{align*}
\left(x_1y_2 - x_1y_3\right)^2 - 2\left(x_1y_2 - x_1y_3\right)\left(y_1x_2 - y_1x_3\right) + \left(y_1x_2 - y_1x_3\right)^2 + 2\left(x_1y_2 - x_1y_3\right)\left(x_2y_3 - x_3y_2\right) + \left(x_2y_3 - x_3y_2\right)^2 &= x_1^2y_2^2 - 2x_1y_2x_1y_3 + x_1^2y_3^2 - 2x_1y_2y_1x_2 + 2x_1y_3y_1x_2 + y_1^2x_2^2 - 2y_1x_2y_1x_3 + y_1^2x_3^2 + 2x_1y_2x_2y_3 - 2x_1y_3x_2y_3 - 2x_1y_2y_1x_3 + 2x_1y_3y_1x_3 + 2x_1y_2x_2y_3 - 2x_1y_3x_2y_3 + x_2^2y_3^2 - 2x_2y_3x_3y_2 + x_3^2y_2^2 \\
&= x_1^2y_2^2 - 2x_1y_2x_1y_3 + x_1^2y_3

To simplify the given expression, we will follow the order of operations (PEMDAS):

1. Distribute the terms inside the parentheses:

\begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) & = x\left(x+x^2+2x^2\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

2. Simplify the terms inside the parentheses:

\begin{align*}
x\left(x+x^2+2x^2\right)-3(x^2-x+2) & = x\left(x+x^2+2x^2\right)-3(x^2-x+2) \\
& = x\left(x+3x^2\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

3. Distribute the x term:

\begin{align*}
x\left(x+3x^2\right)-3(x^2-x+2) & = x^2+3x^3-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

4. Distribute the -3 term:

\begin{align*}
x^2+3x^3-3(x^2-x+2) & = x^2+3x^3-3x^2+3x-6
\end{align*}

5. Combine like terms:

\begin{align*}
x^2+3x^3-3x^2+3x-6 & = 3x^3-2x^2+3x-6
\end{align*}

The simplified expression is:

\boxed{3x^3-2x^2+3x-6}

To compute $(2 \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) \times (3 \mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}),$ we will use the properties of the cross product.

First, we can use the distributive property of the cross product to expand the expression:

\begin{align*}
(2 \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) \times (3 \mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}) &= (2 \mathbf{b}) \times (3 \mathbf{c}) + (2 \mathbf{b}) \times \mathbf{a} - \mathbf{a} \times (3 \mathbf{c}) - \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{a}.
\end{align*}

Since the cross product of any vector with itself is the zero vector, we have $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{a} = \mathbf{0}.$

Now, we can use the scalar multiplication property of the cross product to rewrite the expression as:

\begin{align*}
(2 \mathbf{b}) \times (3 \mathbf{c}) + (2 \mathbf{b}) \times \mathbf{a} - \mathbf{a} \times (3 \mathbf{c}) &= 6 (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) + 2 (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a}) - 3 (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}).
\end{align*}

We are given that $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -7 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix},$ $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix},$ and $\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}.$

We can use these to find $\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c},$ and $\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}$:

\begin{align*}
\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a} &= - \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = - \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -7 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ 7 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix},\\
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c} &= \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix},\\
\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}

Now, we can substitute these values into the expression:

\begin{align*}
6 (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) + 2 (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a}) - 3 (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}) &= 6 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix} + 2 \begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ 7 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} - 3 \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}\\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -42 \\ 108 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ 14 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ 21 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}\\
&= \begin{pmatrix} -18 \\ -49 \\ 96 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}

Therefore, $(2 \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) \times (3 \mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}) = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\ -49 \\ 96 \end{pmatrix}}.$
